enhancement.detailedExplanation:
Section 63 BSA is arguably the most practically critical provision in modern civil and criminal litigation. It replaces and refines the controversial Section 65B of the Indian Evidence Act, 1872, eliminating procedural ambiguities by statutorily incorporating a formal Certificate template directly into the Schedule of the Act.

Under the best evidence rule, a party must produce the original document (primary evidence). In the digital context, producing the original "computer" or "server" is frequently impossible, impractical, or destructive to ongoing systems (e.g., a massive telecommunications server hosting Call Detail Records or a bank’s mainframe). Section 63 resolves this by creating a legal fiction under sub-section (1): copies, printouts, or media extracts are deemed to be documents and are admissible in evidence without producing the original computer.

However, to prevent fabrication, sub-section (2) establishes four cumulative filters:
1. The computer output was produced during a period over which the computer was regularly used to store or process information;
2. Information of that kind was regularly supplied to the computer in the ordinary course of activities;
3. The computer was operating properly throughout the material period (or any period of downtime did not affect the accuracy of the record);
4. The electronic copy reproduces or is derived from information supplied to the computer in ordinary activities.

Sub-section (4) enacts the mandatory condition precedent: the electronic record must be accompanied by a certificate identifying the record, describing the production process, and signed by a person occupying a responsible official position in relation to the operation of the device or the management of the relevant activities. The BSA incorporates an explicit Schedule containing the exact two-part format: Part A for the device custodian, and Part B for the technical/forensic expert. Without this certificate, secondary electronic evidence is legally inadmissible.
